  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 1218 PM CDT TUE SEP 29 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery reveals an upper trough stretching down to\nthe northern Gulf as a ridge builds out to our west. Drier air\nand surface high pressure in place across the region will keep\ndry conditions in place for the next couple of days. The surface\nhigh will begin to scoot east during the day today, which will\nallow for a moist southerly flow to return. As a result we will\nsee a gradual uptick in humidity and temperatures. Chances for\nshowers and storms return to the forecast Friday afternoon as an\nupper trough moves across the Plains. An associated cold front\nwill head our way late in the work week, eventually working\nthrough the region over the weekend. The exact timing remains\nuncertain at this time as there are some signs of it stalling\nover the first half of the weekend before pushing through.\nRegardless, we are looking at medium to high chances for showers\nand storms over the weekend and continuing into early next week\nas southerly flow ushers a healthy plume of tropical moisture\nacross the southeast. Forecast soundings hint at a skinny CAPE\nprofile and a warm cloud depth up to 15kft, suggesting locally\nheavy rainfall is likely with any shower or storm over the\nweekend. Rainfall amounts have increased since our last forecast\nupdate. We are now looking at 3-4\" with isolated pockets over 4\"\nthrough the weekend.\n\nThe front finally pushes through early next week, bringing some\ncooler air back to Central Alabama with high temps topping out in\nthe mid 70s to low 80s and lows in the mid 50s to low 60s.\n\n95/Castillo",
